- Class based components are nothing but regular JS classes extending React.Component.
- e.g.

    class UserClass extends React.Component {}
- like a regular class it has a constructor where we need to get the props using
    super(props)
- state variables are also defined in the constructor using reserved "state" keyword
    this.state = {count: 0}
- to change the state variable we use a function is given by react:
    this.setState({count: count+1})
  here when we update the state, the state variables that are passed will only be changed and the others will be kept as is.

- Lifecycle of React Component:
    => Render Phase
        - constructor
        - render()
            - child rendering
            - child constructor
            - render
            - child lifecycle methods
        - componentDidMount  
    => Updating Phase
        - if any setState() is called render will be called
        - componentDidUpdate
    => Unmount Phase
        - when the component is removed from UI componentWillUnmount will be called

- For child components, 
    > rendering of Parent Component is done first,
    > then rendering of all the components is done first in batch one by one
    > then componentDidMount is run for each one by one
    > lastly componentDidMount of the parent will run

- super(props) is needed to pass props to parent constructor and running the parent constructor for better binding of react code
- async callbacks are not allowed in useEffect hook as it will be against the expectation from react of either return nothing or return a clean up function. secondly this will make inconsistency and confusion in react as to when to perfom the clean up.